1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,900
Lim Peng Siang, the Man Singapore
Called Its Greatest Magnate

2
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:09,912
In July 1936, a reporter for a
Singapore Sunday paper called Lim

3
00:00:09,992 --> 00:00:14,925
Peng Siang one of "the greatest
magnates living in Singapore."

4
00:00:14,925 --> 00:00:19,802
He had built a rice mill and an oil
mill into a group that spanned shipping,

5
00:00:19,882 --> 00:00:22,932
banking and cement, and
for a few decades he was

6
00:00:23,012 --> 00:00:26,925
one of the most important Chinese
businessmen in the colony.

7
00:00:26,925 --> 00:00:30,602
Today his name is almost
unknown, attached to little

8
00:00:30,682 --> 00:00:34,800
more than a minor quay near
the port his ships once used.

9
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:40,351
Lim Peng Siang was born in Amoy
(now Xiamen), in Fujian province,

10
00:00:40,431 --> 00:00:44,850
in 1872, the sixth son of
the merchant Lim Ho Puah.

11
00:00:44,850 --> 00:00:49,332
His mother was the daughter of
Wee Bin, founder of Wee Bin & Co.,

12
00:00:49,412 --> 00:00:54,805
one of the major Straits Chinese shipping
firms of the mid-nineteenth century,

13
00:00:54,885 --> 00:00:58,675
and Lim Ho Puah himself went
on to run that same firm.

14
00:00:58,675 --> 00:01:03,013
Lim Peng Siang grew up, in other
words, in a family already deep

15
00:01:03,093 --> 00:01:07,650
in Singapore's shipping trade
before he built anything of his own.

16
00:01:07,650 --> 00:01:10,975
His own start was smaller and different.

17
00:01:10,975 --> 00:01:14,338
In 1904, he and his brother Lim Peng Mau

18
00:01:14,418 --> 00:01:17,867
founded Ho Hong Co. on a loan of $50,000,

19
00:01:17,947 --> 00:01:22,250
going into rice and coconut
oil rather than ships.

20
00:01:22,250 --> 00:01:26,904
Ho Hong's rice mill processed
up to 100 tons of parboiled

21
00:01:26,984 --> 00:01:32,302
rice a day, and its oil mill,
powered by a 250-horsepower engine,

22
00:01:32,382 --> 00:01:36,700
could turn out up to 130
tons of coconut oil a week.

23
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:40,717
It was a modest, industrial
beginning: milling, not trading,

24
00:01:40,797 --> 00:01:45,850
and machinery bought outright rather
than capital borrowed against a name.

25
00:01:45,850 --> 00:01:50,000
That name mattered more
than Lim probably expected.

26
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,196
In 1911, Wee Bin & Co.,
the firm his father had run

27
00:01:54,276 --> 00:01:58,300
and his grandfather had
founded, was liquidated.

28
00:01:58,300 --> 00:02:02,501
At the public auction of its
assets, Lim Peng Siang bought four

29
00:02:02,581 --> 00:02:06,782
of its steamships, the firm's
most valuable remaining property,

30
00:02:06,862 --> 00:02:11,075
and folded them into a new
shipping department inside Ho Hong.

31
00:02:11,075 --> 00:02:15,161
Two years later he formalised
the business as the Ho Hong

32
00:02:15,241 --> 00:02:19,619
Steamship Co. Limited, and the
fleet grew quickly: by 1926 it

33
00:02:19,699 --> 00:02:23,419
ran fourteen vessels, some
of them named, fittingly,

34
00:02:23,499 --> 00:02:28,250
after Singapore places, Tanjong
Rhu and Tanjong Kling among them.

35
00:02:28,250 --> 00:02:31,602
Buying a bankrupt rival's
ships at auction is an

36
00:02:31,682 --> 00:02:35,106
ordinary enough business
move, but this was also,

37
00:02:35,186 --> 00:02:38,475
quite literally, buying
back the family trade.

38
00:02:38,475 --> 00:02:43,650
The firm he was absorbing had been
founded by his own grandfather.

39
00:02:43,650 --> 00:02:48,100
Ho Hong kept adding
pieces through the 1910s.

40
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:52,079
Lim co-founded the Chinese
Commercial Bank in 1912,

41
00:02:52,159 --> 00:02:58,685
with $2,000,000 in issued capital, and in
1917 he helped found Ho Hong Bank itself,

42
00:02:58,765 --> 00:03:02,744
alongside Lim Boon Keng,
a name readers of our post

43
00:03:02,824 --> 00:03:07,600
on Song Ong Siang will recognise
as Song's own collaborator.

44
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:12,207
Ho Hong Bank opened with
$1,750,000 in paid-up capital,

45
00:03:12,287 --> 00:03:18,854
and expanded fast beyond Singapore,
opening branches in Batavia and Palembang,

46
00:03:18,934 --> 00:03:23,450
in the Dutch East Indies,
within its first few years.

47
00:03:23,450 --> 00:03:26,882
By the early 1920s a
Portland cement works,

48
00:03:26,962 --> 00:03:31,618
also under the Ho Hong name,
was turning out cement at Ulu

49
00:03:31,698 --> 00:03:35,700
Pandan with some $3,000,000
in capital behind it.

50
00:03:35,700 --> 00:03:39,128
Rice, oil, shipping,
banking and cement under one

51
00:03:39,208 --> 00:03:42,780
roof was an unusual
combination for a Chinese-owned

52
00:03:42,860 --> 00:03:46,647
business in colonial Singapore,
where firms more often

53
00:03:46,727 --> 00:03:50,450
specialised or spread through
simple trading houses.

54
00:03:50,450 --> 00:03:54,550
A 2023 academic study of
Lim's enterprises argues that

55
00:03:54,630 --> 00:03:58,420
businessmen like him were
already building modern,

56
00:03:58,500 --> 00:04:03,528
technically ambitious industrial
companies well before the postwar

57
00:04:03,608 --> 00:04:08,175
period usually credited with
Singapore's industrialisation.

58
00:04:08,175 --> 00:04:12,150
Lim's standing outside Ho
Hong grew alongside it.

59
00:04:12,150 --> 00:04:16,988
He served as president of the
Singapore Chinese Chamber of

60
00:04:17,068 --> 00:04:22,585
Commerce from 1913 to 1916, sat
on the Chinese Advisory Board from

61
00:04:22,665 --> 00:04:27,842
1921 until the Japanese invasion
in 1941, and chaired the Hong

62
00:04:27,922 --> 00:04:32,925
Kong Fujian Chamber of Commerce
with his brother from 1930.

63
00:04:32,925 --> 00:04:37,181
He was made a Justice of the Peace
and was offered a seat on the

64
00:04:37,261 --> 00:04:41,800
Legislative Council more than once,
which he turned down each time.

65
00:04:41,800 --> 00:04:45,932
During the First World War, he
and the businessman Lee Choon

66
00:04:46,012 --> 00:04:50,575
Guan jointly donated a fighter
aircraft to the British Air Force.

67
00:04:50,575 --> 00:04:55,206
The plane, one of several bought
by public subscription in Malaya,

68
00:04:55,286 --> 00:04:59,275
was named Malaya No. 6 and
carried the donors' own names,

69
00:04:59,355 --> 00:05:02,425
Choon Guan Peng Siang,
painted on its side.

70
00:05:02,425 --> 00:05:06,230
It was this kind of standing,
decades in the building,

71
00:05:06,310 --> 00:05:09,826
that the Sunday paper was
pointing to in 1936 when

72
00:05:09,906 --> 00:05:13,575
it called him one of
Singapore's greatest magnates.

73
00:05:13,575 --> 00:05:16,550
The Depression broke the momentum.

74
00:05:16,550 --> 00:05:21,511
Fish and rubber prices collapsed
across Malaya in the early 1930s,

75
00:05:21,591 --> 00:05:26,018
and Ho Hong was not spared:
Lim sold his shipping interests

76
00:05:26,098 --> 00:05:31,059
to the Straits Steamship Company
for $900,000, reorganised the oil

77
00:05:31,139 --> 00:05:35,875
mills in 1931, and closed the
cement works the following year.

78
00:05:35,875 --> 00:05:38,875
The heavier blow came from banking.

79
00:05:38,875 --> 00:05:43,089
Three years earlier, in 1929,
the Chinese Commercial Bank

80
00:05:43,169 --> 00:05:46,553
had rebuilt its own
premises on Chulia Street,

81
00:05:46,633 --> 00:05:51,676
a reinforced-concrete building of
five storeys, going up alongside a

82
00:05:51,756 --> 00:05:56,275
similar new building for the
Oversea-Chinese Bank next door.

83
00:05:56,275 --> 00:05:59,411
Neither bank's board can
have known it at the time,

84
00:05:59,491 --> 00:06:01,807
but they were modernising for a future

85
00:06:01,887 --> 00:06:04,850
that would fold them into
the same institution.

86
00:06:04,850 --> 00:06:09,226
On the 31st of October 1932,
the Chinese Commercial Bank

87
00:06:09,306 --> 00:06:13,443
and Ho Hong Bank, the two
banks Lim had helped build,

88
00:06:13,523 --> 00:06:17,262
merged with the Oversea-Chinese
Bank to form the

89
00:06:17,342 --> 00:06:22,275
Oversea-Chinese Banking Corporation,
now known simply as OCBC.

90
00:06:22,275 --> 00:06:26,289
The merger is remembered
today as the work of Tan Ean

91
00:06:26,369 --> 00:06:30,383
Kiam and Lee Kong Chian,
the men who led it and whose

92
00:06:30,463 --> 00:06:34,325
names survive in OCBC's
own institutional history.

93
00:06:34,325 --> 00:06:39,956
Lim Peng Siang, who had put up two of the
three banks that made the merger possible,

94
00:06:40,036 --> 00:06:43,300
is not part of that story
as it is usually told.

95
00:06:43,300 --> 00:06:48,339
He had already been profiled, at
length, in Song Ong Siang's 1923 book,

96
00:06:48,419 --> 00:06:52,304
One Hundred Years' History
of the Chinese in Singapore,

97
00:06:52,384 --> 00:06:55,700
written while Ho Hong
was still near its peak.

98
00:06:55,700 --> 00:07:00,208
That record, and the 1936
newspaper profile that followed it,

99
00:07:00,288 --> 00:07:04,275
are largely what a researcher
has to work with today.

100
00:07:04,275 --> 00:07:08,559
A 2023 study of his career notes
plainly that Lim Peng Siang

101
00:07:08,639 --> 00:07:13,142
has drawn far less scholarly
attention than contemporaries such

102
00:07:13,222 --> 00:07:17,069
as Tan Kah Kee, even though
his group was, for a time,

103
00:07:17,149 --> 00:07:21,950
one of the larger Chinese-owned
industrial concerns in the region.

104
00:07:21,950 --> 00:07:26,038
Lim Peng Siang died in
Singapore on the 21st

105
00:07:26,118 --> 00:07:30,475
of March 1944, during
the Japanese Occupation.

106
00:07:30,475 --> 00:07:32,947
His name lives on in Peng Siang Quay,

107
00:07:33,027 --> 00:07:37,223
a short stretch of waterfront
near Tanjong Pagar, not far from

108
00:07:37,303 --> 00:07:40,475
the harbour his steamships
once sailed out of.

109
00:07:40,475 --> 00:07:44,479
Why it matters today: OCBC,
formed in 1932 out of three

110
00:07:44,559 --> 00:07:49,084
separate Chinese-owned banks,
is one of Singapore's oldest and

111
00:07:49,164 --> 00:07:53,539
most prominent institutions,
but its own prehistory has been

112
00:07:53,619 --> 00:07:58,075
flattened into the story of the
men who arranged the merger.

113
00:07:58,075 --> 00:08:01,576
Two of the three banks that
went into it belonged to

114
00:08:01,656 --> 00:08:05,364
a man once called one of
Singapore's greatest magnates,

115
00:08:05,444 --> 00:08:08,750
and almost nobody today
could tell you his name.
